<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luís de Camões</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1524–<a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Luís Vaz de Camões, Camoens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Luís Vaz de Camões was Portugal's greatest poet. He lost an eye fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and spent many years as a soldier and official in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the Far East before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, where his epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/os-lusiadas">Os Lusíadas</a> was published in 1572. He also wrote lyric poems and plays. He died in poverty in 1580, and the day of his death, 10 June, is Portugal's national day.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luis-de-camoes.jpg" alt="Luís de Camões" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernão Gomes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cam%C3%B5es,_por_Fern%C3%A3o_Gomes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Portuguese culture produced the Manueline style of the Jerónimos Monastery and the Tower of Belém, the epic Os Lusíadas of <b>Luís de Camões</b>, published in 1572, and advances in navigation and cartography.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Renaissance humanists identified Portugal with Lusitania, and <b>Luís de Camões</b> called his national epic of 1572 Os Lusíadas, ‘the Lusiads’, after the mythical Lusus; ‘Lusophone’ still describes the Portuguese-speaking world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1560" aria-label="Lisbon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4323 775 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Luís de Camões"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1045h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1045h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.14&amp;year=1560">What was here in 1560? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O you, the boldest of the nations, fir'd By daring pride, by lust of fame inspir'd, Who, scornful of the bow'rs of sweet repose, Through these my waves advance your fearless prows…&#8221;</p><footer>The Lusiads (1572), canto 5 &#183; tr. William Julius Mickle, 1776 &#183; spoken by Adamastor, the giant of the Cape of Storms, to Vasco da Gama's fleet &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/32528/pg32528-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
